    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-1794",
      "problem": "Sketch the graph of the function $y=(x+3)^2.$ What are the coordinates of the minimum point?",
      "worked_solution": "In general, the graph of $y=(x-h)^2$ looks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$ but translated $h$ units right. The vertex of $y=(x-h)^2$ is located at $(h,0).$\n\nIn the given function $y=(x+3)^2,$ we have $h=-3.$ To see this clearly, we can write the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x+3)^{2} \\\\ y & =(x-(-3))^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the parabola $y=x^2$ after being translated $3$ units *left*. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218419/assets/bffdacd979eaa77a7a64364840ab318bcc4a1c39ca78137146e82f37540e02e4.png)\n\nThe vertex is located at $(-3,0),$ and these are the coordinates of the minimum point.",
